    "title": "Tax deadline is here. How to file an extension with the IRS",
    "text": "April 14, 2026, 5:03 p.m. ETForgot to pay Uncle Sam?The Internal Revenue Service, IRS reminds taxpayers that they can request an extension to file their federal income tax return by Oct. 15, 2026 in order to avoid penalties and interest before the deadline.The filing deadline for 2025 tax returns is April 15, 2026, and that day is upon us.The extension only provides extra time to file, not additional time to pay so you must still pay by April 15. But paying later adds interest and penalties.To avoid or minimize penalties taxpayers should estimate their total tax liability, subtract any payments already made, and pay the remaining balance by the deadline according to IRS.gov.Below is how taxpayers can file an extension.When's the last day to file taxes 2025The deadline for federal 2025 tax returns is Tuesday, April 15, 2026. 2025 New Jersey tax returns are due the same day the federal return is due.How to file an extensionThe IRS has 3 ways for taxpayers to request an extension to Oct. 15.Pay online and select “extension.”Pay what you owe using an online payment option and check the box that you are paying as part of filing for an extension. You don’t have to file a separate extension form and you’ll receive a confirmation number of your extension for your records.Use IRS Free File to electronically request an automatic tax-filing extension.Request an extension by mail: Free File Fillable Forms. Taxpayers can complete and submit Form 4868, Application for Automatic Extension of Time To File U.S. Individual Income Tax Return, electronically, regardless of income.File Form 4868 by mail. Taxpayers can complete and mail Form 4868, Application for Automatic Extension of Time To File U.S. Individual Income Tax Return, to the address listed in the form instructions.Ways to file for freeFree File - If your adjusted gross income is $89,000 or less. If higher, you can use Free File Fillable Forms.Free tax services for military and veteransIRS-certified volunteers – If you earn $69,000 or less, have a disability, are 60 years or older or need language support.Where's my refund?Eligible tax payers can track the status of their refund three ways:By creating an Your Online Account , downloading the mobile app IRS2Go app and the online tool Where's My Refund?.To check the status you will need your Social Security or individual taxpayer ID number (ITIN), what your filing status is and the exact refund amount. The IRS updates these applications daily overnight.",
